  "body": "## Finding description and impact\n\nThe injectSweepAndCall function in TrailsRouter is designed to “sweep” a caller’s full balance in a given token (or ETH), inject that amount into a call’s calldata, and execute the call against a target contract. It is clearly written under the assumption that the router’s code runs in the context of a user wallet via delegatecall, where address(this) refers to the wallet, not the router itself.\nHowever, the function is declared as a normal external function without any onlyDelegatecall or equivalent restriction. When called directly on the router contract, it transfers tokens from msg.sender into address(this) (the router), and subsequent internal logic uses the router’s own balance via _getSelfBalance / address(this).\nThis mismatch between intended execution context (delegatecall into a wallet/shim) and actual allowed execution context (direct external call on the router) causes the router to accumulate and control user funds. The core faulty assumption is that “this function will only ever execute in a context where address(this) is a wallet (or shim) owned by the user”; in reality, it is fully callable on the router implementation itself, turning a stateless routing component into a stateful asset custodian and significantly increasing blast radius for any other bug.\n\nIntended context:\nThe router is meant to be used via delegatecall from a smart wallet or a shim contract.\nIn that model:\naddress(this) inside the router code resolves to the wallet/shim address, not the router contract.\nToken and ETH balances tracked by _getSelfBalance(token) are the wallet’s balances.\nThe router is effectively “pure logic” with no long-term funds; all balances belong to the calling wallet.\nActual context allowed by the code:\n```javascript\nfunction injectSweepAndCall(\n    address token,\n    address target,\n    bytes calldata callData,\n    uint256 amountOffset,\n    bytes32 placeholder\n) external payable {\n    uint256 callerBalance;\n\n    if (token == address(0)) {\n        callerBalance = msg.value;\n        if (callerBalance == 0) revert NoEthSent();\n    } else {\n        callerBalance = _getBalance(token, msg.sender);\n        if (callerBalance == 0) revert NoTokensToSweep();\n        _safeTransferFrom(token, msg.sender, address(this), amount);\n    }\n\n    _injectAndExecuteCall(token, target, callData, amountOffset, placeholder, callerBalance);\n}\n```\n\nimpact:\nAsset Custody & Fund Loss:\nUsers may unknowingly send funds to ROUTER expecting a stateless routing behavior.\nIf TARGET is misconfigured, malicious, or fails:\nFunds remain in ROUTER without a clear path to recovery.\nAlso the previous bug(injectAndCall Lets Anyone Spend Router-Held Funds due to missing  Delegatecall Guard) reported gets strong anyone can  withdraw tokens from ROUTER, the attacker can drain these accumulated balances.\n\n## Recommended mitigation steps\n\nrestrict injectSweepAndCall to delegatecall-only\nUse the existing onlyDelegatecall modifier from DelegatecallGuard on injectSweepAndCall, and add a short doc comment making the assumption explicit.\n\n```javascript\n\n/// @inheritdoc ITrailsRouter\n/// @dev Must only be used via delegatecall from a wallet/shim context so that\n///      `address(this)` refers to the wallet, not the router implementation.\nfunction injectSweepAndCall(\n    address token,\n    address target,\n    bytes calldata callData,\n    uint256 amountOffset,\n    bytes32 placeholder\n) external payable onlyDelegatecall {\n    uint256 callerBalance;\n\n    if (token == address(0)) {\n        callerBalance = msg.value;\n        if (callerBalance == 0) revert NoEthSent();\n    } else {\n        callerBalance = _getBalance(token, msg.sender);\n        if (callerBalance == 0) revert NoTokensToSweep();\n        _safeTransferFrom(token, msg.sender, address(this), callerBalance);\n    }\n\n    _injectAndExecuteCall(token, target, callData, amountOffset, placeholder, callerBalance);\n}\n```",
  "proof_of_concept": "forge test --match-test test_PoC_injectSweepAndCall_accumulatesRouterBalance -vvv\n\n```javascript\n\n// SPDX-License-Identifier: MIT\npragma solidity ^0.8.30;\n\nimport {Test} from \"forge-std/Test.sol\";\nimport {TrailsRouter} from \"src/TrailsRouter.sol\";\nimport {MockERC20} from \"test/mocks/MockERC20.sol\";\n\n\ncontract PartialDrainTarget {\n    MockERC20 public token;\n    uint256 public lastRequested;\n    uint256 public lastPulled;\n\n    constructor(address _token) {\n        token = MockERC20(_token);\n    }\n\n    /// @dev Pulls only 10% of the requested amount from msg.sender to the receiver.\n    function partialDeposit(uint256 amount, address receiver) external {\n        lastRequested = amount;\n        uint256 toPull = amount / 10;\n        lastPulled = toPull;\n        require(token.transferFrom(msg.sender, receiver, toPull), \"transferFrom failed\");\n    }\n}\n\n/// @notice PoC for CRITICAL-4: `injectSweepAndCall` directly callable, causing the router\n///         to accumulate user tokens and act as an unintended custodian.\ncontract TrailsRouterInjectSweepAndCallPoC is Test {\n    TrailsRouter internal router;\n    MockERC20 internal token;\n    PartialDrainTarget internal partialTarget;\n\n    address internal attacker;\n\n    bytes32 internal constant PLACEHOLDER =\n        0xdeadbeefdeadbeefdeadbeefdeadbeefdeadbeefdeadbeefdeadbeefdeadbeef;\n\n    function setUp() public {\n        router = new TrailsRouter();\n        token = new MockERC20(\"Test Token\", \"TST\", 18);\n        partialTarget = new PartialDrainTarget(address(token));\n        attacker = makeAddr(\"attacker\");\n\n        // Give attacker some tokens they will \"sweep\" via injectSweepAndCall\n        token.mint(attacker, 1_000e18);\n    }\n\n    /// @notice PoC: attacker directly calls injectSweepAndCall; router ends up\n    ///         holding the majority of the attacker's tokens, demonstrating that\n    ///         the router becomes an unintended asset custodian.\n    function test_PoC_injectSweepAndCall_accumulatesRouterBalance() public {\n        // Attacker starts with all tokens, router/target with zero\n        uint256 initialRouterBalance = token.balanceOf(address(router));\n        uint256 initialAttackerBalance = token.balanceOf(attacker);\n        uint256 initialTargetBalance = token.balanceOf(address(partialTarget));\n\n        assertEq(initialRouterBalance, 0, \"router should start with zero tokens\");\n        assertEq(initialTargetBalance, 0, \"target should start with zero tokens\");\n        assertEq(initialAttackerBalance, 1_000e18, \"attacker funded for PoC\");\n\n        // Attacker approves the router so it can pull their full balance\n        vm.prank(attacker);\n        token.approve(address(router), type(uint256).max);\n\n        // Craft calldata: the first argument is a PLACEHOLDER for the amount,\n        // which injectSweepAndCall will replace with the attacker's swept balance.\n        bytes memory callData =\n            abi.encodeWithSignature(\"partialDeposit(uint256,address)\", PLACEHOLDER, attacker);\n\n        // Direct call to the router (NOT via delegatecall / wallet context)\n        // This causes:\n        // 1. Router to transferFrom(attacker -> router) the full attacker balance.\n        // 2. Router to approve the target for that full balance.\n        // 3. Target to pull only 10% of the amount, leaving 90% on the router.\n        vm.prank(attacker);\n        router.injectSweepAndCall(address(token), address(partialTarget), callData, 4, PLACEHOLDER);\n\n        // Final balances and state after the direct call\n        uint256 finalRouterBalance = token.balanceOf(address(router));\n        uint256 finalAttackerBalance = token.balanceOf(attacker);\n        uint256 finalTargetBalance = token.balanceOf(address(partialTarget));\n\n        // Router has now accumulated a large token balance it should never hold\n        // in the intended delegatecall-only design.\n        assertGt(finalRouterBalance, 0, \"router should now hold user tokens\");\n\n        // Attacker has lost most of their balance; only a fraction was returned\n        // via the PartialDrainTarget, demonstrating user fund loss.\n        assertLt(finalAttackerBalance, initialAttackerBalance, \"attacker lost tokens to router\");\n\n        // The target may or may not hold tokens depending on receiver logic,\n        // but the key invariant is broken: router is now a token custodian.\n        assertEq(finalTargetBalance, 0, \"target does not retain tokens in this PoC\");\n    }\n}\n\n\n\n\n```",
